From 40392d3dd16b4d5e09a6dc3b4f9e63a84e9ba10a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: RivonClaw CI <ci@rivonclaw.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 22:34:11 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] vendor(openclaw): give the cron fence a Windows process
 identity

Why:
- `prepareCronRunReceiptClaim` requires a non-null process start time, but
  `shared/pid-alive.ts#getFileLockProcessStartTime` implements Linux procfs and
  macOS `ps` only and returns null on every other platform. On Windows every
  cron execution therefore fails immediately with "cron run cannot acquire a
  durable fence without process start identity", for both scheduled runs and
  manual run-now, because both enter through the same admission claim.
- Upstream already ships `infra/windows-port-pids.ts#readWindowsProcessStartTimeSync`
  for exactly this purpose, and both `infra/gateway-lock.ts` and
  `node-host/node-worker-process-identity.ts` already wrap the shared helper
  with this win32 branch. The cron receipt store was simply missed.
- The staleness comparison is switched to the same reader so the persisted and
  observed owner identities stay in one unit; fixing only the claim would make
  Windows write a creation timestamp and then compare it against null, silently
  disabling PID-reuse detection.
- Fixed at the cron call site rather than in `shared/pid-alive.ts` to keep the
  blast radius on cron: the shared helper also backs file locks, startup
  migration checkpoints, and stale-lock adjudication, whose Windows semantics
  are out of scope here, and `shared/` deliberately does not depend on `infra/`.

Removal:
- Drop this patch once the pin contains an upstream `getFileLockProcessStartTime`
  that resolves a Windows process start time (or an equivalent cron-side fix).
  Verify by running the vendor regression below on pristine vendor with the
  platform stubbed to win32; it must still acquire a fence.

Tests:
- src/cron/store/run-receipt-store.windows-start-time.test.ts
- apps/desktop/src/gateway/vendor-windows-cron-fence.sentinel.test.ts
